```python
def update_profile_photo(user_id, new_photo_url):
    write_to_primary(user_id, new_photo_url)
    return {"status": "ok"}

def get_profile(user_id, replica):
    return replica.read(user_id)   # might be a replica the write hasn't reached yet
```
A user updates their photo, gets a success response, then immediately reloads their own profile and sees the *old* photo because the read landed on a replica the write hasn't reached yet. That's a read-after-write bug — annoying and visible, even though the system will converge within milliseconds.
